Interview questions with model answers
What is the difference between AI, ML and deep learning? AI is the broad goal of machines performing intelligent tasks. ML is a subset where systems learn patterns from data instead of following hand-written rules. Deep learning is ML using multi-layer neural networks.
What is an LLM? A large neural network, usually a transformer, trained on huge amounts of text to predict the next token. That simple objective gives it broad language ability.
Is Claude the same as ChatGPT? They are different model families from different companies, Anthropic and OpenAI. Both are LLM-based assistants, but they differ in training, behavior and tooling. Never claim the products are the same.
What is generative AI? Models that produce new content such as text, code or images. LLMs are the text and code branch of it.
What is an AI agent? An LLM in a loop that plans, calls tools, observes the results and continues toward a goal, with some autonomy over the steps.
Workflow vs agent? A workflow follows steps defined in code, so it’s predictable. An agent decides its own steps, so it’s flexible but harder to control and test. Use the simplest option that works.
What is MCP? The Model Context Protocol is an open standard that lets AI applications connect to tools, data and prompts through servers. It is plumbing and adds no intelligence itself.
Are AI, ML, agents and MCP interchangeable? No. They sit at different layers: the field, the technique, the behavior pattern and the connection standard.
Why might a QA engineer be valuable in AI teams? AI systems are non-deterministic and fail in subtle ways. Evals, regression testing, edge cases, release gates and flakiness are core QA skills, and they are exactly what production AI needs.
Why is non-determinism a testing challenge? The same input can produce different outputs, so exact-match assertions fail. You test with graded evals, repeated runs, thresholds and LLM-as-judge instead.
